{:edb/db-before-t 1330 :edb/db-after-t 1331 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534643 50 #inst "2026-10-08T03:04:20.450Z" 13194139534643 true] [17592186171672 1145 "Let $z$ be a $6$th root of unity. This means that\n\n$$\nz^{6} = 1\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $re^{\\textrm{i}\\theta}$. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\mid 1 \\mid = 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\arg(1) = 0\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n1 = e^{\\textrm{i}(0 + 2k\\pi)} = e^{2k\\pi \\textrm{i}}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\nz^{6} = e^{2kπ\\textrm{i}}⟹z = (e^{2kπ\\textrm{i}})^{1/6} = e^{kπ\\textrm{i}/3}\n$$\n\nThe principal root corresponds to $k=1$, and is therefore equal to $e^{\\pi \\textrm{i}/3}$." 13194139534643 false] [17592186171672 1145 "Let $z$ be a $6$th root of unity. This means that \n\n$$\nz^{6} = 1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $re^{\\textrm{i}\\theta}.$ Notice that $|1| = 1$ and $\\arg(1) = 0.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n1 = e^{\\textrm{i}(0 + 2k\\pi)} = e^{2k\\pi \\textrm{i}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{6}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}}\\,⟹\\,z=({e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}})^{1/6}={e}^{k\\pi \\text{i}/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal root corresponds to $k=1,$ and is therefore equal to $e^{\\pi \\textrm{i}/3}.$" 13194139534643 true] [17592186171673 1145 "Geometrically, the $4$th roots of unity have the following properties:\n\n- all of them lie on the unit circle centered in the origin,\n\n- one of the roots is $1,$ corresponding to the point $(1,0)$ on the complex plane,\n\n- the roots form a square (regular $4$-sided polygon).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980994/assets/9111f21143412d791b0195722da4f6d461444f386efd9baf609c70a4fe709fd8.png)" 13194139534643 true] [17592186171673 1145 "Geometrically, the $4$th roots of unity have the following properties:\n\n- all of them lie on the unit circle centered in the origin,\n- one of the roots is $1$, corresponding to the point $(1,0)$ on the complex plane,\n- the roots form a square (regular $4$-sided polygon).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3400/Source/Images/28513be60912977c95ecf4ec773d3792.png)" 13194139534643 false] [17592186171674 1100 "Find the root of unity highlighted in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980994/assets/7ae59d6d770f5cb0ef10a42c6bc9e5851fa48b1d122417ce78155a8dcf17be6a.png)" 13194139534643 true] [17592186171674 1100 "Find the root of unity highlighted in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3400/Source/Images/98e9ea0ab66a999ba39a7d107f156d9f.png)" 13194139534643 false] [17592186171674 1145 "The $n$th roots of unity lie at the vertices of a regular $n$-gon inscribed inside the unit circle in the complex plane. One of the vertices of this regular $n$-gon must lie at the point $(1,0)$.\n\nSince there are $7$ points in our diagram, we need to find a $7$th root of unity.\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $re^{\\textrm{i}\\theta}$. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\mid 1 \\mid = 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\arg(1) = 0\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n1 = e^{\\textrm{i}(0 + 2k\\pi)} = e^{2k\\pi \\textrm{i}}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\nz^{7} = e^{2kπ\\textrm{i}}⟹z = (e^{2kπ\\textrm{i}})^{1/7} = e^{2kπ\\textrm{i}/7}\n$$\n\nTo find the index $k$ of our root of unity, we enumerate the roots in the diagram counter-clockwise, starting with the root at $(1,0)$ corresponding to $k=0$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3400/Source/Images/047d35f0458fccba0e9e329b3fa9f8c2.png)\n\nSo, the required root corresponds to $k=5$. Substituting this into the expression for roots, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nz_{5} = e^{2(5)π\\textrm{i}/7} = e^{10π\\textrm{i}/7}\n$$" 13194139534643 false] [17592186171674 1145 "The $n$th roots of unity lie at the vertices of a regular $n$-gon inscribed inside the unit circle in the complex plane. One of the vertices of this regular $n$-gon must lie at the point $(1,0).$\n\nSince there are $7$ points in our diagram, we need to find a $7$th root of unity.\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $re^{\\textrm{i}\\theta}.$ Notice that $|1| = 1$ and $\\arg(1) = 0.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n1 = e^{\\textrm{i}(0 + 2k\\pi)} = e^{2k\\pi \\textrm{i}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{7}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}}\\,⟹\\,z=({e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}})^{1/7}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}/7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the index $k$ of our root of unity, we enumerate the roots in the diagram counter-clockwise, starting with the root at $(1,0)$ corresponding to $k=0,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980994/assets/730b0c3c63799b9101ce89d38e3d96c10bc0e217b967506393ea67757ecf48d7.png)\n\n \n\nSo, the required root corresponds to $k=5.$ Substituting this into the expression for roots, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}_{5}={e}^{2(5)\\pi \\text{i}/7}={e}^{10\\pi \\text{i}/7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534643 true] [17592186171844 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300810 13194139534643 true] [17592186171844 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300866 13194139534643 true] [17592186171844 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300873 13194139534643 true] [17592186171844 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300880 13194139534643 true] [17592186171845 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300817 13194139534643 true] [17592186171845 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300824 13194139534643 true] [17592186171845 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300831 13194139534643 true] [17592186171845 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300838 13194139534643 true] [17592186171846 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300845 13194139534643 true] [17592186171846 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300852 13194139534643 true] [17592186171846 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300859 13194139534643 true] [17592186172313 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534643 true] [17592186172313 1145 "Geometrically, the $3$rd roots of unity have the following properties:\n\n- all of them lie on the unit circle centered in the origin,\n\n- one of the roots is $1,$ corresponding to the point $(1,0)$ on the complex plane,\n\n- the roots form an equilateral triangle (regular $3$-sided polygon).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980994/assets/c70d25ac3a5ddb2d6765ef8a22f5e9774c9be06dd909d7079c3a361157881bb5.png)" 13194139534643 true] [17592186172358 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534643 true] [17592186172358 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980994/assets/0c592ccfd6ea92e0f0181d5dfe4804cc0dba800f468d9fb4e9d96cef2be72be4.png)\n\nFind the $6$th root of unity highlighted in the diagram above." 13194139534643 true] [17592186172358 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3400/Source/Images/q-211001.png)\n\nFind the $6$th root of unity highlighted in the diagram above." 13194139534643 false] [17592186172358 1145 "The $n$th roots of unity lie at the vertices of a regular $n$-gon inscribed inside the unit circle in the complex plane. One of the vertices of this regular $n$-gon must lie at the point $(1,0).$\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }.$ Notice that $|1|=1$ and $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(1)=0.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n1={e}^{\\text{i}(0+2k\\pi )}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{6}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}}\\,⟹\\,z=({e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}})^{1/6}={e}^{k\\pi \\text{i}/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the index $k$ of our root of unity, we enumerate the roots in the diagram counter-clockwise, starting with the root at $(1,0)$ corresponding to $k=0,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980994/assets/ff5d1682ca8d142ad4cf73b9a5c996164653d940157efd695d1da13d34900404.png)\n\n \n\nSo, the required root corresponds to $k=4.$ Substituting this into the expression for roots, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}_{4}={e}^{(4)\\pi \\text{i}/3}={e}^{4\\pi \\text{i}/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534643 true] [17592186172359 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534643 true] [17592186172359 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980994/assets/0a23346ac28172521a3230a949607422efe77e2803973ac42e4255f321c3a1a0.png)\n\nFind the root of unity highlighted in the diagram above." 13194139534643 true] [17592186172359 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3400/Source/Images/q-211004.png)\n\nFind the root of unity highlighted in the diagram above." 13194139534643 false] [17592186172359 1145 "The $n$th roots of unity lie at the vertices of a regular $n$-gon inscribed inside the unit circle in the complex plane. One of the vertices of this regular $n$-gon must lie at the point $(1,0).$\n\nSince there are $5$ points in our diagram, we need to find a $5$th root of unity.\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }.$ Notice that $|1|=1$ and $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(1)=0.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n1={e}^{\\text{i}(0+2k\\pi )}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{5}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}}\\,⟹\\,z=({e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}})^{1/5}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}/5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the index $k$ of our root of unity, we enumerate the roots in the diagram counter-clockwise, starting with the root at $(1,0)$ corresponding to $k=0,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980994/assets/53c8822760ceb51c1d1bd685947dbfefa7dfdc3490027db62cbcaf5d9aa24335.png)\n\n \n\nSo, the required root corresponds to $k=2.$ Substituting this into the expression for roots, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}_{2}={e}^{2(2)\\pi \\text{i}/5}={e}^{4\\pi \\text{i}/5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534643 true] [17592186172591 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534643 true] [17592186172591 1145 "Let $z$ be a $12$th root of unity. This means that \n\n$$\n{z}^{12}=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }.$ Notice that $|1|=1$ and $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(1)=0.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n1={e}^{\\text{i}(0+2k\\pi )}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{12}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}}\\,⟹\\,z=({e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}})^{1/12}={e}^{k\\pi \\text{i}/6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal root corresponds to $k=1,$ and is therefore equal to ${e}^{\\pi \\text{i}/6}.$" 13194139534643 true] [17592186300810 1017 #edb/ref 17592186300811 13194139534643 true] [17592186300810 1089 "q-107494" 13194139534643 true] [17592186300810 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534643 true] [17592186300810 1100 "Find the principal $22$th root of unity." 13194139534643 true] [17592186300810 1113 #uuid "e02786bb-01fd-59ab-afe0-3626bcf912d7" 13194139534643 true] [17592186300810 1145 "Let $z$ be a $22$th root of unity. This means that \n\n$$\n{z}^{22}=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }.$ Notice that $|1|=1$ and $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(1)=0.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n1={e}^{\\text{i}(0+2k\\pi )}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{22}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}}\\,⟹\\,z=({e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}})^{1/22}={e}^{k\\pi \\text{i}/11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal root corresponds to $k=1,$ and is therefore equal to ${e}^{\\pi \\text{i}/11}.$" 13194139534643 true] [17592186300811 1000 #edb/ref 17592186300812 13194139534643 true] [17592186300811 1000 #edb/ref 17592186300813 13194139534643 true] [17592186300811 1000 #edb/ref 17592186300814 13194139534643 true] [17592186300811 1000 #edb/ref 17592186300815 13194139534643 true] [17592186300811 